---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import burials from "../data/burials.json";
const nudgee = burials.filter((b) => b.place.includes("Nudgee"));
const other = burials.filter((b) => !b.place.includes("Nudgee"));
---
<Base title="Nudgee" eyebrow="Nudgee Catholic Cemetery, Brisbane"
      dek="Thirteen of this family are buried in one cemetery on the northern side of Brisbane — including all four of Michael Rocco Mazza's grandparents' generation who died in Australia.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Nudgee Cemetery, beside the Holy Cross church at Banyo, has been the Catholic
    burying ground of northern Brisbane since the 1860s. It is where post-war Italian Brisbane went,
    and it is why this archive keeps meeting the same ground from a different direction: <strong>five
    Falco of Arienzo</strong> lie here too, and <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheFalco/">that
    archive</a> found them independently.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">Two families with nothing in common in Italy — one from
    Campania, three from Calabria and Sicily — buried within the same fence in Queensland. That is
    not a family connection. It is what a migration looks like from the outside.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The thirteen</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Born</th><th class="m">Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{nudgee.sort((a, b) => (a.died || 9999) - (b.died || 9999)).map((b) => (
          <tr><td><a href={u("/people/" + b.slug)}>{b.who}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{b.born || "—"}</td><td class="m">{b.died || "—"}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">The tree records the cemetery but not the plot, the section or, for most of them,
    the date. Nudgee's burial register would supply all three, and would confirm or correct every
    death year in this table. It has not been consulted yet.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Buried elsewhere</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Place</th><th class="m">Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{other.map((b) => (
          <tr><td><a href={u("/people/" + b.slug)}>{b.who}</a></td>
              <td>{b.place}</td><td class="m">{b.died || "—"}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px">Three of them are at <strong>Arneytown, New Jersey</strong>
    — the Brigadier General William C. Doyle Veterans Memorial Cemetery. Burial there requires
    American military service. The Polistena who went to New York in the first wave produced at least
    two servicemen, and the archive knows nothing else about them.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
